+# Cause and Fix for Soft 404 on Note Pages
+
+## Cause
+
+Every page on `fieldnotes.example` is a single static HTML shell (`site/index.html`) rendered client-side by `site/app.js`, which fetches data from the same-domain API under `/v1/`:
+- Note pages (`/n/<slug>`) fetch `GET /v1/notes/<slug>`
+- The home page (`/`) fetches `GET /v1/notes?limit=50`
+
+On 2026-09-07, `Disallow: /v1/` was added to `site/robots.txt` to stop crawlers from making `GET` requests to `POST`-only API endpoints (like `/v1/notes/{slug}/reactions` and `/v1/auth/token`) and logging 404/405 errors.
+
+While regular browsers ignore `robots.txt` and load the pages normally (returning HTTP 200 with full content), **Googlebot obeys `robots.txt` for subresource `fetch()` calls when rendering JavaScript**. When Googlebot renders `/n/<slug>`, it blocks the fetch to `/v1/notes/<slug>`, causing `fetch()` to reject. The `catch` block in `site/app.js` then renders:
+
+> `NOT FOUND — No note lives at this address. Back to all notes`
+
+Because the server returns `HTTP 200` for `/n/<slug>` while the rendered page displays a "NOT FOUND" error page, Google Search Console classifies every newly crawled note page as a **Soft 404** (while notes crawled prior to September 7 remained indexed).
+
+## Fix
+
+Update `site/robots.txt` to allow the public read API endpoints (`/v1/notes` and `/v1/notes/{slug}`) while keeping the general `/v1/` block (which covers `/v1/me`, `/v1/me/drafts`, and `/v1/auth/token`) and explicitly blocking the `POST`-only sub-route `/v1/notes/*/reactions` using a longer, more specific rule (Google's `robots.txt` evaluator uses longest-match precedence):
+
+```txt
+User-agent: *
+Allow: /v1/notes
+Disallow: /v1/notes/*/reactions
+Disallow: /v1/
+Disallow: /admin/
+
+Sitemap: https://fieldnotes.example/sitemap.xml
+```
+
+### How Google evaluates these rules (longest match wins):
+- `/v1/notes?limit=50` & `/v1/notes/<slug>` → matches `Allow: /v1/notes` (10 chars) over `Disallow: /v1/` (4 chars) → **Allowed**
+- `/v1/notes/<slug>/reactions` → matches `Disallow: /v1/notes/*/reactions` (21 chars) over `Allow: /v1/notes` (10 chars) → **Disallowed**
+- `/v1/me`, `/v1/me/drafts`, `/v1/auth/token` → matches `Disallow: /v1/` (4 chars) → **Disallowed**
+- `/admin/*` → matches `Disallow: /admin/` → **Disallowed**
